# <%= current_page.data.title %>

This is a proposed follow-up story, not an available workflow. We need one approach
for testing file-transfer Python functions in `modernisation-platform-environments`,
rather than a separate workflow for each delivery pattern.

## What we want

As a file-transfer maintainer, I want Python changes tested automatically and failures
reported to Slack, so that I can fix problems before deploying them.

## Proposed approach

Keep the runner in `integration-hub`. The platform team owns the infrastructure
workflows, so adding a trigger there needs their agreement.

Prefer a platform-approved cross-repository event when a pull request changes Python
functions, shared Python code, tests or dependency files under
`terraform/environments/integration-hub-file-transfer`. If that trigger is not
available, a scheduled watcher in `integration-hub` can inspect changed pull-request
heads and main. It should record tested revisions and avoid repeat runs. We need to
agree the polling interval and how that state is stored before implementation.

Resolve the revision automatically and record the exact commit in the result. Keep a
manual rerun option for investigations; do not maintain a commit hash in the workflow.
Use a suite manifest to list each function's test command, working directory and
pinned development dependencies. Changes to shared code should run all affected
suites. A changed function without tests should be reported explicitly.

Run tests without AWS credentials or Slack secrets. Pull-request code is untrusted;
use read-only repository access and isolated runners. A separate trusted reporting job
should send the result to Slack without executing code from the tested checkout.

## Acceptance criteria

- A relevant Python change triggers tests automatically; unrelated changes do not.
- The runner covers multiple function suites, including pickup and shared dispatch
  code, and reports missing test coverage rather than silently skipping it.
- Results identify the repository, pull request, exact commit and failed suite.
- A failed run posts a concise message to the agreed Slack channel with a link to the
  logs. Do not include file contents, credentials or raw test output in that message.
- Repeated polling does not duplicate tests or failure alerts for the same revision.
  An explicit rerun remains possible.
- Fork pull requests cannot access Slack or AWS credentials. Notification failures
  remain visible in the workflow results.
- The result is easy to find from the infrastructure pull request. Agree the GitHub
  App permissions needed to publish a check before making it a merge requirement.
- Test an intentional failure, its Slack alert, a passing run and a manual rerun.

Before implementation, agree the trigger with the Modernisation Platform team and
choose the Slack channel and secret owner. This work is separate from the existing
AWS smoke-test harness, which checks deployed infrastructure rather than unit tests.
